          "text": "# Spain: Co-ordinated protection for women facing intersecting discrimination\n\n## Policy objective\n\nSpain should turn its extensive policy commitments into consistent, victim-centred protection, support and access to justice across national, regional and local levels. Article 7 of the Istanbul Convention requires comprehensive, co-ordinated measures that address the specific circumstances and barriers facing women exposed to multiple, intersecting forms of discrimination.\n\n## Findings and implementation gaps\n\nThe policy architecture has strengthened. The 2023-2027 Multi-Annual Joint Plan establishes a national–regional co-operation framework and a reference catalogue of minimum services. The 2022-2025 State Strategy covers all Convention forms of violence and recognises intersectionality; population-specific strategies also address violence against women.\n\nYet protection and support remain difficult to access. GREVIO highlights disproportionate victimisation of migrant women, women with disabilities and rural women. Professionals often address one discrimination factor rather than interacting disadvantages, particularly affecting migrant women, women in prostitution, LBTI women and women with disabilities.\n\nImplementation varies across regions. Co-ordination remains insufficient between government levels and autonomous communities. Civil society identifies unclear State Pact implementation priorities; the 19 Co-ordination Units against Violence against Women need further resources. Evaluation is insufficiently systematic, thorough and independent, while NGOs, especially those supporting women facing intersecting discrimination, lack institutionalised channels for regular participation.\n\n## Priorities for policymakers\n\n**Make intersectionality operational.** Implement measures tailored to victims’ specific needs, recognising multiple, interacting barriers rather than treating discrimination as a single characteristic. Apply this approach to service delivery, protection and justice, keeping victims’ rights central.\n\n**Strengthen territorial consistency.** Use the Joint Plan and reference catalogue to secure minimum services across administrative levels. Clarify State Pact implementation priorities, reinforce co-ordination between levels and autonomous communities, and resource the co-ordination units to perform their role effectively.\n\n**Evaluate regularly and independently.** Build on the State Strategy’s indicators and regular evaluation arrangements, and the Ministry of Equality’s computerised evaluation system. Define indicators and stakeholder responsibilities beforehand, assess policy impacts using reliable data, and identify implementation difficulties requiring correction.\n\n**Institutionalise NGO participation.** Ensure regular involvement of organisations working directly with victims, including women exposed to intersecting discrimination, in policy making, monitoring and evaluation. Their practical expertise should inform assessment of whether commitments translate into accessible protection and support.\n\nAccountability matters: the State Pact’s first evaluation came in 2023, after missing indicators and unclear responsibilities had prevented comprehensive parliamentary scrutiny. Consistent implementation therefore requires not only policy commitments, but also demonstrable effectiveness for women whose overlapping disadvantages obstruct access to assistance.",
